{
  "dom_id": "step-q181743",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "$$\nA={5,7,9,11,…},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):\n\nSince the function $f:B\\to A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =2 \\\\  & =7-5 \\\\  & =9-7 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =5+(n-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5+2n-2 \\\\  & =2n+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA={2n+3∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ | $⋯$ | $2n+3$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=2n+3\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
